                ‘ mechanism (
                %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#DescartesMechanism"}> LectHistPhil-Descartes
                );
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza"}> Spinoza
                ‘s
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determination_%28Logik%29"} omnis determinatio est negatio
                (Hegel’s
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DeterminateNegation"} determinate negation
                %a{:href => "#EL91ZusatzA"} EL§91ZusatzA
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza"}> Spinoza
                ‘s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza%27s+system"} system
                of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
                (
                %a{:href => "#1287"}> §1287
                );
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Descartes"}> Descartes
                ‘
                %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-ontological/"} ontological argument
                for a notion that implies its own objective existence (
                %a{:href => "#1530b"}> §1530b
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#1530c"} §1530c
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Descartes"}> Descartes
                ‘s and
                = succeed "\u2019s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fichte"} Fichte
                %em
                  schlechthin unbedingter
                  %a{:href => "Grundlage+der+gesamten+Wissenschaftslehre#ErsterGrundsatz"} erster Grundsatz
                (
                %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#DescartesSchlechthingGewiss"}> LectHistPhil-Descartes
                ,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#FichteIchDasErste"} LectHistPhil- Fichte
                %em Notion
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#1291b"} §1291b
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fichte"}> Fichte
                ‘s principle that philosophy should be
                %em deriving
                determinations (
                %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#FichteVorzugWissenschaft"}> LectHistPhil-Fichte
                ) by “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/speculation"}> speculation
                ” (
                %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#DescartesNonSpeculative"}> LectHistPhil-Descartes
                )
          %p
            Also there is some serious
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structuralism
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#1065a"} §1065a
            = succeed "." do
              %em Science of Logic
          %p#Hermeticism
            Where Hegel speaks of his system as being
            %em science
            “Wissenschaft” (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Phenomenology+of+Spirit#Vorrede5"} PdGVorrede§5
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            and where he refers to its
            %em
              %a{:href => "#DialektischeMethode"} dialectic method
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#62"} §62
            %a{:href => "#63"} §63
            this is to claim that the
            %em process
            is indeed being systematically derived and proven (
            %a{:href => "#50"}> §50
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} EncPreface1stEd
            %a{:href => "#PdGPreface15"} PdGPreface15
            as the
            %em Arbeit des Begriffs
            (
            %a{:href => "Phenomenology+of+Spirit#ArbeitDesBegriffs"}> PhenVorr
            ) – but by “supersensuous inner intuition”
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#1786a"} §1786a
            %em observation = speculari
            is what the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/speculative+philosophy"} speculative philosophy
            for this school of thought refers to. Therefore Hegel is speaking to some extent in a mystic or gnostic tone, revealing truths by the way of a seer, following the similarly mysterious second part of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Plato"} Plato
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Parmenides+dialogue"} Parmenides dialogue
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#357"} §357
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mysticism"} mysticism
            (
            %a{:href => "#3"}> §3
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Enc82d"} Enc§82d
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Russell45"} Russell 45
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Copleston71"} Copleston 71
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Stanfield14"} Stanfield 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gnosticism"} gnosticism
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bauer35"}> Bauer 1835
            ) or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermeticism"} hermeticism
            (
            %a{:href => "#Magee01"}> Magee 01
            ). Indeed, in
            %a{:href => ""} PhenGeistVorrede
            and in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#8"} §8
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#9"} §9
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#10"} §10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#11"} §11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#50"} §50
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#51"} §51
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#52"} §52
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#53a"} §53a
            %a{:href => "#53b"} §53b
            Hegel explains that the
            %em Phenomenology of the Spirit
            is to be regarded as the “first part of the system” in that it establishes the necessary spiritual background to embark on the intellectual project of the
            = succeed "," do
              %em Science of Logic
            %em Science of Logic
            needs to previously have worked itself through the stages all the way starting from sensual perception, consciousness, reason, via moral and intellectual education, through various stages of religion, to finally achieve the stage of “absolute knowledge”. Apparently not actually expecting that all his readers prepare themselves accordingly, Hegel at times pauses to comment on the incomprehensibility of his very text (e.g. the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#IncomprehensibilityOfTheBeginning"} Incomprehensibility of the beginning
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#1648"} §1648
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#PN261b"} PN§261b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meister+Eckhart"} Meister Eckhart
            %a{:href => "Meister+Eckart#UnverhuellteWahrheit"} did
            in his texts on the union of the soul with god. Hegel explicitly acknowledges accomplishments of gnosticism and mysticism for philosophy (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"}> EncPreface2ndEd
            ), but indicates there that he regards his system as more refined. In the
            %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+Philosophy+of+Religion#Preliminaries"} preliminaries
            to his
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+the+Philosophy+of+Religion"} Lectures on the Philosophy of Religion
            Hegel states that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            and
            %em religion
            have the same subject, fall together, both are “worship” of the “eternal truth in its very objectivity”, just by different means.
          %p
            In any case, this means that, in an ironic meta-contradiction, the harder Hegel tries, by his own account, to make philosophy a
            = succeed "," do
              %em science
            %em logical
            and to root it in
            = succeed "," do
              %em observation
          %p
            Accordingly, after a phase of great popularity in the philosophical community of the 19th century (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/German+idealism"}> German idealism
            ”,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#316"} §316
            %a{:href => "Georg+Hegel#PerceptionOfHegelsPhilosophy"} here
            for examples) been rejected and outright ridiculed, famously so by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"} Bertrand Russell
            in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Logic+as+the+Essence+of+Philosophy"} Logic as the Essence of Philosophy
            and in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+History+of+Western+Philosophy"} A History of Western Philosophy
            %a{:href => "#Grassmann1844"} Grassmann 1844, p. xv
            whose introduction however speaks entirely in a Hegel-like tone, then later for instance
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.gleichsatz.de/b-u-t/trad/carn1a.html#dies"} Carnap 32
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+philosophy"} analytic philosophy
            defined itself – in what is known as the “
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertrand_Russell%27s_views_on_philosophy#Analytic_philosophy"}> revolt against idealism
            ” – to a large extent in opposition to Hegel’s
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+idealism"} absolute idealism
            (and more generally to “continental philosophy”), and aimed, in contrast, for undisputable clarity of argument, optimally by use of formalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "#1798"} §1798
              formal thinking lays down for its principle that contradiction is unthinkable; but as a matter of fact the thinking of contradiction is the essential moment of the Notion.
          %p
            However, there is more to formal logic than plain predicate logic.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} Foundational
            systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            (which happens to have its roots in (
            %a{:href => "type+theory#Russell08"}> Russell 08
            )) subsume first-order logic but also allow for richer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            such as notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            stand a good chance of usefully formalizing recurring themes of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            (of opposites) in philosophy was observed in the 1980s (
            %a{:href => "#Lambek82"}> Lambek 82
            ) notably by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Rodin14"} Rodin 14
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere95"} Lawvere 95
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Grassmann1844"} Grassmann 1844
            = succeed "," do
              %em Logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            (sublation) and of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general%2C+concrete+general+and+concrete+particular"} abstract general, concrete general and concrete particular
            as well as the concepts of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"} objective logic
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subjective+logic"} subjective logic
            as such (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere94b"}> Law94b
            ) have an accurate, useful and interesting formalization in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            matches well under this translation from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            also proposed formalizations in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            of various terms appearing prominently in Hegel’s
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} Philosophy of Nature
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+or+extensive+quantity"} intensive or extensive quantity
            and of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://philosophy.stackexchange.com/a/9814/5473"} Have professional philosophers contributed to other fields in the last 20 years?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+philosophy"} analytic philosophy
            seems to have little chance of achieving.
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere92"}> Lawvere 92
              \: It is my belief that in the next decade and in the next century the technical advances forged by category theorists will be of value to dialectical philosophy, lending precise form with disputable mathematical models to ancient philosophical distinctions such as general vs. particular, objective vs. subjective, being vs. becoming, space vs. quantity, equality vs. difference, quantitative vs. qualitative etc. In turn the explicit attention by mathematicians to such philosophical questions is necessary to achieve the goal of making mathematics (and hence other sciences) more widely learnable and useable. Of course this will require that philosophers learn mathematics and that mathematicians learn philosophy.
          #nPOVRemark.un_remark
            %h6#npov nPOV
            %p
              Therefore, while going through Hegel’s text, this page here attempts to spell out as much as seems possible the translation of the system to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type-theoretic
