          %blockquote
            %p
              All
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p
              Individual
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p Hence
          %blockquote
            %p
              Individual
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi E
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi E
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  f &amp; \colon B \longrightarrow A
                  \\\
                  b &amp; \colon E \longrightarrow B
                  \\\
                  f b &amp; \colon E \longrightarrow A
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            %strong
              The figure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B-B-A
          %p
            Analogously, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for
          %blockquote
            %p
              Some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            %p
              All
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p Hence
          %blockquote
            %p
              Some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            (all in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %munder
                            %mo ×
                            %mi C
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  B_1 \underset{C}{\times} B_2 &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; B_2 &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; A
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  B_1 &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; C
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Or rather, this composite map will factor through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %munder
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \underset{C}{\times} A
            and the resulting map to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            gives ‘Some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
            is
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p We may also treat negative propositions, such as
          %blockquote
            %p
              No
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
          %p
            which may be represented by an arrow
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %munder
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \underset{C}{\times} B_2 \to 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %munder
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \underset{C}{\times} A
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %munder
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \underset{C}{\times} B_2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %p This corresponds to the syllogism
          %blockquote
            %p
              No
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            %p
              All
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
          %p Hence,
          %blockquote
            %p
              No
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h4#the_method_absolute_idea The method (absolute Idea)
          %p
            What is not present in such bare homotopy type theory is determination of further qualities of types. For instance for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetically
            formalizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            one needs that types have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean-topological+infinity-groupoid"} topological
            and moreover
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} differential geometric
            qualities to them. Some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} externalizations
            of homotopy type theory exhibit these, others do not. We now consider adding axioms to homotopy type theory that narrow it in on those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            that do exhibit further quality in addition to the pure being of types.
          %p
            %strong Moments
          %p
            A general abstract way to express a kind of quality carried by types is to posit a
            %em projection
            operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            that projects out the
            = succeed "." do
              %em moment of pure such quality
          %p
            For instance for formalizing realistic physics one needs to determine
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} bosonic
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
            moments (we come to this
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Externalization"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            operation that projects every space of fields to its purely bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/body"} body
            (lemma
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SequenceOfSites"}
            below).
          %p
            Generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a type, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc X
            is to be the result of projecting out some pure quality of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc X  \simeq \bigcirc \bigcirc X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive"} constructive
            %em moment projection
            is an operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            on the type system together with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ◯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \bigcirc X
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ◯
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc(X \to \bigcirc X)
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc X \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} \bigcirc \bigcirc X
          %p
            In categorical semantics this means essentially that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            is an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
            on the type system
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            Alternatively we may ask for a comparison map the other way around,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box X \longrightarrow X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(\Box X \longrightarrow X)
            is an equivalence. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            this means essentially that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
          #Moments.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %em moment
              on (or in) a type system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              is
            %ul
              %li
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \colon \mathbf{H} \to \mathbf{H}
            %p or
            %ul
              %li
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo □
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \colon \mathbf{H} \to \mathbf{H}
            %p Given a moment, we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo ,
                        %mo □
                    %mo ↪
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{H}_{\bigcirc, \Box} \hookrightarrow \mathbf{H}
            %p for the inclusion of its image, which we think of as the collection of those types that exhibit the moment purely (conversely these define the kind of moment as whatever quality it is that they all exhibit purely).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This is a language construct natural and familiar also from the point of view of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
            %p
              Further, it makes sense to refer to moments
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box,\;\bigcirc
              also as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
              or just
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
              for short, and speak of type theory equipped with such operators as
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{\Box}
              are the
              = succeed "-_" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
              %em .
          #MomentsAndSimilarity.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              A moment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              may be thought of as encoding a concept of
              = succeed ":" do
                %em similarity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              may be different but “similar with respect to
              = succeed "-quality\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              are equivalent:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi similar
                      %mo ◯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (X \,similar_{\bigcirc}\, Y)
                    \\coloneqq
                    (\bigcirc(X) \,= \, \bigcirc(Y))
            %p
              (where on the right we have an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
              of the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
          %p
            Example of this are made explicit below as example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SimilarityWithRespectToPureBeing"}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ShapeAsAConceptOfSimilarity"}
          #bigcircpureObjectsAreEMObjects.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{\bigcirc}
              is equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg-Moore category
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
          %p
            This is a standard fact in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "idempotent+monad#AlgebrasForAnIdempotentMonad"} idempotent monad – Algebras
          #PurityForNonIdempotent.num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#bigcircpureObjectsAreEMObjects"}
              means that we may naturally make sense of “pure qualtity” also for (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"}> co-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
              that are not
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+a+monad"} algebras
          %p
            A single moment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreted
            as most anything, since it is not further determined yet. One now observes that there is an intrinsic, self-propelling way to further determine such abstract moments, by asking for their
            %em opposite
            and for their
            %em negative
            moments.
          %p
            %strong Opposites
          #UnityOfOpposites.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (unity of opposites)
            %p
              The
              %em opposite
              of a moment
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Moments"}
              is, if it exists, another moment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              with it,
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  either
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                  to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo □
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
                  and such that there is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                          %mo □
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mo ◯
                        %mover
                          %mover
                            %mo ↪
                            %mo ⟵
                          %mo ↪
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathbf{H}_{\Box}\simeq H_{\bigcirc}
                        \\stackrel{\hookrightarrow}{\stackrel{\longleftarrow}{\hookrightarrow}}
                        \\mathbf{H}
                %p which we denote by
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo □
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo ◯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\Box \dashv \bigcirc
              %li
                %p
                  or
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                  to it with
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                          %mo □
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                          %mo ◯
                        %mover
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟵
                            %mo ↪
                          %mo ⟵
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathbf{H}_{\Box}\simeq \mathbf{H}_{\bigcirc}
                        \\stackrel{\longleftarrow}{\stackrel{\hookrightarrow}{\longleftarrow}}
                        \\mathbf{H}
                %p which we denote by
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo □
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\bigcirc \dashv \Box
                        \\,.
            %p
              (We always display
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              on top and its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              beneath.)
            %p
              We say that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              itself is the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                      %mi moment
                    %mo □
                    %mover
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mi unity
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi of
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mi opposites
                    %mo ◯
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mi opposite
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi moment
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\stackrel{moment}{} \Box \stackrel{unity \atop {of\,opposites}}{\dashv} \bigcirc \stackrel{opposite\;moment}{}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              an opposition of moments, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UnityOfOpposites"}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  of the form
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo □
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo ◯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \dashv \bigcirc
                  defines an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ◯
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level of a topos
              %li
                %p
                  of the form
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo □
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
                  defines a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bireflective+subcategory"} bireflective subcategory
                  (in the sense of:
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective
                  and
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
          %p
            It is fairly familiar from the practice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            express oppositions. The following example is drawn from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"} arithmetic
            and is meant to illustrate this in a familar context, but the actual examples that we will be concerned with are more fundamental.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Consider the two inclusions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi even
                    %mo ,
                    %mi odd
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} even, odd \colon (\mathbb{Z},\lt ) \hookrightarrow (\mathbb{Z},\lt)
              of the even and the odd integers, i.e. the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \mapsto 2 n
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \mapsto (2n+1)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi odd
                        %mo ⟵
                      %mover
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi even
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{Z}
                    \\stackrel{\overset{even}{\hookrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\longleftarrow}{\underset{odd}{\hookrightarrow}}}
                    \\mathbb{Z}
            %p
              Both are adjoint to the operation of forming the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi floor
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} floor
              of the result of dividing by two, this is right adjoint to the inclusion of even numbers, and left adjoint to the inclusion of odd numbers.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                              %mi even
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                              %mi odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\stackrel{even}{} &amp; \Box &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc &amp; \stackrel{odd}{}
                    }
          %p
            This has been considered in (
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Aufhebung#Law00"}> Lawvere 00
            )
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              Consider the inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon (\mathbb{Z}, \lt) \hookrightarrow (\mathbb{R}, \lt)
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              into the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+orders"} linear orders
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              given by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ceiling
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ceiling
              and a right adjoint given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi floor
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} floor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ceiling
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ι
                    %mi ceiling
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ceiling \coloneqq \iota ceiling
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
              and the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Floor
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ι
                    %mi floor
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Floor \coloneqq \iota floor
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %em moment of integrality
              in an real number, but in opposite ways, each real number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in \mathbb{R}
              sits in between its floor and celling
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Floor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi Ceiling
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Floor(x) \lt x \lt Ceiling(x)
                    \\,.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                              %mi ceiling
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                              %mi floor
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\stackrel{ceiling}{} &amp;\bigcirc &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box &amp; \stackrel{floor}{}
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p This example highlights that:
          #TheTwoKindsOfOpposites.num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p There is an opposition between the two kinds of opposition here:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo □
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box \dashv \bigcirc)
                  – Here are two different opposite “pure moments” .
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo □
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\bigcirc \dashv \Box)
                  – Here is only one pure moment, but two opposite ways of projecting onto it.
          %p#DeterminateNegation
            %strong Determinate negation
          %blockquote
            %p EL92Zusatz Negation ist das, was wir Grenze heißen. Etwas ist nur in seiner Grenze und durch seine Grenze das, was es ist.
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:10px 10px 10px 15px;"}
            %img{:src => "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Cup_or_faces_paradox.svg/450px-Cup_or_faces_paradox.svg.png", :width => "300"}/
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box X
            is a pure moment found inside
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em complement moment
            or its
            %em negative
          #NegativeMoment.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em negative
              of a comonadic moment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              is what remains after taking away the piece of pure
              = succeed "-quality," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofiber"} cofiber
              of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi cofib
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{\Box}(X) \coloneqq cofib(\Box X \to X)
                    \\,.
          %p
            The intuitive meaning suggests to ask whether this kind of negation of determinations is faithful in that there is no
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\Box}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mover
                    %mo □
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box \overline{\Box} \simeq \ast
                  \\,.
          %p
            In general there is no reason for this to be the case. But if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            also has an opposite in the sense of def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UnityOfOpposites"}
          %p
            Consider the case of an opposition of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheTwoKindsOfOpposites"}
            then both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            express the same pure moment, just opposite ways of projecting onto it. Therefore in this situation it makes sense to alternatively ask that there is no
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\Box}
          #CompleteOpposition.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Given a unity of opposite moments
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UnityOfOpposites"}
              %em determinate negation
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              both restrict to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-type"} 0-type
              and such that there
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                        %mo *
                        %mo ≃
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \ast \simeq \ast
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo □
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mo ◯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box  \longrightarrow \bigcirc
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epi
          #AccuracyOfNegatives.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For an opposition with determinate negation, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompleteOpposition"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-types"} 0-types
              there is no
              = succeed "-moment" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              = succeed "-moment:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\bigcirc \overline{\Box} \simeq \ast
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Given that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibers"} cofibers
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompleteOpposition"}
              gives that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi cofib
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi cofib
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\bigcirc \overline{\Box} X
                    \=
                    \\bigcirc cofib(\Box X \to X)
                    \\simeq
                    cofib(\Box X \to \bigcirc X)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now the second condition and the fact that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epiness
              is preserved by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              say that this result receives an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              from the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
          %p
            The proof of prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AccuracyOfNegatives"}
            depends crucially on the restriction to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-types"} 0-types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable types
            the intuition that
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\Box}
          #TheHexagon.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              For opposite moments of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UnityOfOpposites"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+hexagon"} hexagons
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ◯
                              %mo ¯
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo □
                              %mo ¯
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ◯
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo □
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                            %mover
                              %mo □
                              %mo ¯
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \overline{\bigcirc} X &amp;&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;&amp; \overline{\Box} X
                    \\\
                    \&amp;\nearrow&amp; &amp;\searrow&amp; &amp; \nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \\overline{\bigcirc} \Box X &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \bigcirc \overline{\Box} X
                    \\\
                    \&amp;\searrow&amp; &amp;\nearrow &amp; &amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; \Box X &amp;&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;&amp; \bigcirc X
                    }
            %p are homotopy exact in that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  both squares are homotopy Cartesian, hence are
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+squares"} fracture squares
              %li
                %p
                  the boundary sequences are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+homotopy+fiber+sequences"} long homotopy fiber sequences
            %p
              In particular every stable type is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} fibered direct sum
              of its pure
              = succeed "-moment" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              = succeed "-moment:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo □
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\Box}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◯
                        %mover
                          %mo □
                          %mo ¯
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \simeq (\bigcirc X) \underset{\bigcirc \overline{\Box} X}{\oplus} (\overline{\Box} X)
                    \\,.
          %p
            In this form this has been highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeNikolausVoelkl13"}> Bunke-Nikolaus-Völkl 13
            ) in the context of our prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentialHexagon"}
            below. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+hexagon"} differential hexagon
            for the proof.
          %p
            %strong Accidence
          #Localization.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Say that a moment
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              %em exhibited
              by a type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
              is equivalently
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+localization"} homotopy localization
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi loc
                      %mi J
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\bigcirc \simeq loc_{J}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This implies in particular that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi J
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc J \simeq \ast
          %p
            %strong Progression
          %p
            We have seen how to formalize determination of qualities of types together with their opposite and their negative determination. But so far these determinations are abstract in that when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreting
            them in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            they could come out as all kinds of very different-natured (co-)monads. What is missing is something that bases these determinations on a concrete ground with respect to which they would gain actual meaning.
          %p Indeed, there are natural ways in which determinations of qualities may progress from given ones to further ones: on the one hand a given unity of oppositions may itself have an opposite and hence exhibit a higher order “opposition of oppositions”, on the other hand a given unity of oppositions may be “resolved” inside one new quality, which then in turn may have its own opposite and negative in turn, and so on.
          %p
            %strong Progression I: Higher order opposition
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
            moment (i.e. an interpretation of the moment in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            of the moment, not a choice to made. Abstractly we may specify that moments proceed to further moments this way by positing further adjoints.
          #HigherOrderOpposition.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (opposition of unities of oppositions)
            %p
              Given one opposition
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \dashv \bigcirc
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◊
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge \dashv \Box
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◊
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⊣
                              %mrow
                                %mi unity
                                %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                              %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi opposition
                                %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi of
                                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                    %mi unities
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⊣
                              %mrow
                                %mi unity
                                %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\lozenge &amp;\overset{unity\;2}{\dashv}&amp; \Box
                    \\\
                    \\bot &amp;\stackrel{opposition \atop {of\;unities}}{}&amp; \bot
                    \\\
                    \\Box &amp;\underset{unity\;1}{\dashv}&amp; \bigcirc
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              (Indeed, an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
              is equivalently an adjunction of adjunctions, see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "adjoint+triple#AsAdjunctionOfAdjunctions"} here
          %p
            In principle this may go on, but in models one finds that there are essentially no examples with a fourth adjoint that do not degenerate to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidextrous
            situation where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◊
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge \simeq \bigcirc
          %p
            This shows further how oppositions serve to further determine moments: while a bare
            = succeed "-operator" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
          %p
            %strong Progression II: Resolution of oppositions
          %p There is another way for a system of moments to proceed, not by adding further oppositions, but by resolving them.
          #ResolutionOfOpposition.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              %strong (resolution of unity of opposites)
            %p
              Given an essential subtopos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \dashv \bigcirc
              then one may ask if it sits inside a bigger essential subtopos, we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Box_2 &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_2
                    \\\
                    \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                    \\\
                    \\Box_1 &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_1
                    }
            %p
              to indicate that the image of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_1
              is contained in the image of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ◯
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_2
              is at a
              %em
                higher
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
              or in a
              %em higher sphere
              than
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ◯
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_1
            %p
              If in addition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_1 \lt \bigcirc_2
              then this means that the opposing moments of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_1 \dashv \bigcirc_1
              both are of purely
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_2
              nature, and hence we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_2
              %em resolves
              or
              %em lifts
              or
              %em sublates
              or
              %em
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
                of
              this (unity of) oppostions. We might indicate this by:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Box_2 &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_2
                    \\\
                    \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                    \\\
                    \\Box_1 &amp; &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_1
                    }
            %p
              Dually there may be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; \Box_2 &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                    \\\
                    \\Box_1 &amp;\dashv&amp; &amp; \bigcirc_1
                    }
          %p Notice that for oppositions of the other form, given
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ◯
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo □
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ◯
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo □
                            %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\bigcirc_2 &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box_2
                  \\\
                  \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \\bigcirc_1 &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box_1
                  }
          %p
            then resolution in the sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_2 \Box _1 \simeq \Box_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_2 \bigcirc_1 \simeq \bigcirc_1
            is automatic.
          %p
            These two kinds of progression, higher order opposition, def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherOrderOpposition"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ResolutionOfOpposition"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ◊
                            %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo □
                            %mi b
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ◊
                            %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo □
                            %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo □
                            %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ◯
                            %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots  &amp;\dashv&amp; \vdots
                  \\\
                  \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\lozenge_b &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box_b
                  \\\
                  \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \\lozenge_a &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box_a
                  \\\
                  \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\Box_a &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc_a
                  }
          %h4#ObjectiveLogic Objective Logic
          %p
            By the above discussion, we are led to add to homotopy type theory the axiom that there are various moments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            In the existing literature on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            it is tradition to consider unspecified idempotent (co-)monads to the formal system and have them acquire the intended meaning only via a specific choice of interpretation in a model. But here we are after developing genuine theory that works across all its possible interpretations, and hence we want an axiomatic determination of moments.
          %p
            We observe now that there is a canonical starting point of two opposing moments that are secretly present in plain homotopy type theory. This hence constitutes a ground from which naturally a progression, as
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Progression"} above
          %h5#TheGround The ground
          %p
            %strong The beginning
          %p We observe now that in plain type theory already by itself carries one non-trivial unity of opposites.
          %p
            There is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
          %p
            Every type has a unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to \ast
            to that. Hence there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            which sends every type to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            and this is a moment
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigcirc = \ast
          %p
            according to def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Moments"}
          #SimilarityWithRespectToPureBeing.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              Every type is similar to every other, in the formal sense of def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MomentsAndSimilarity"}
              = succeed "-moment." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#906"} §906
          %p
            Dually there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
          %p
            The empty type is such that it has a unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to X
            to any other type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo =
                  %mi ∅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box = \emptyset
                  \\,.
          %p It is immediate that:
          #InitialOpposition.num_defn
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              In plain homotopy type theory there is a unity of opposites, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UnityOfOpposites"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\emptyset \dashv \ast
                    \\,.
          %p
            We also call this the
            = succeed "." do
              %em initial opposition
          #AspectsOfTheInitialOpposition.num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p It may be suggestive to think of this initial opposition in one of the following ways.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The initial opposition of prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InitialOpposition"}
                  is (leaving
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
                  notationally implicit) the adjunction between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
                  over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                  given by the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mi ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\underset{\emptyset}{\sum}(-)
                        \\;\vdash\;
                        \\underset{\emptyset}{\prod}(-)
                        \\,.
              %li
                %p
                  The initial opposition of prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InitialOpposition"}
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊣
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
                  = succeed "-adjunction" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\left(
                        (-) \times \emptyset
                        \\right)
                        \\;\dashv\;
                        \\left(
                        \\emptyset \to (-)
                        \\right)
                        \\,.
            %p
              On the other hand, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊣
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
              = succeed "-adjunction" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mo *
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    (-) \times \ast
                    \\right)
                    \\;\dashv\;
                    \\left(
                    \\ast \to (-)
                    \\right)
            %p is the identity moment, in opposition with itself:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    id \dashv \id
                    \\,.
            %p
              This trivially resolves the initial opposition. Moreover, the negative, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegativeMoment"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id
              is
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi id
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{id} = \ast
            %p So that we find
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mover
                              %mo *
                              %mo ¯
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ∅
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mover
                              %mi id
                              %mo ¯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    id &amp;\dashv&amp; id &amp; = \overline{\ast}
                    \\\
                    \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                    \\\
                    \\emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp; = \overline{id}
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              From this perspective it seems as if alternatively
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\id \dashv \id)
              could be referred to as the initial opposition.
            %p
              Notice for completeness that the negative, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegativeMoment"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"} maybe monad
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Moments"}
              since it is not
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
          %p Now we may find a progression of further moments by considering the resolution of this unity and then opposites to this resolution, and so forth.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \dashv \bigcirc
              contains the initial opposition of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InitialOpposition"}
              as the minimal one:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo □
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mo ◯
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∨
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ∅
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊣
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Box &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc
                    \\\
                    \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                    \\\
                    \\emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p
            We are to demand that this provides a resolution, def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ResolutionOfOpposition"}
            of the initial opposition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \dashv \ast
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InitialOpposition"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◯
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ∅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigcirc \emptyset \simeq \emptyset
                  \\,.
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            this says equivalently that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ◯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box \dashv \bigcirc)
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} dense subtopos
          %p
            %strong Double negation
          #InitialOpposition.num_defn
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %em smallest
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} dense subtopos
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              is that of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+object"} local types
              with respect to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♯
                      %mo ≔
                      %msub
                        %mi loc
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp \coloneqq loc_{\neg \neg}
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone02"}> Johnstone 02, corollary A4.5.20
            ).
          %p
            Therefore we may add the demand that the resolution of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset \dashv \ast)
            be by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi loc
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} loc_{\neg \neg}
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere91"}> Lawvere 91, p. 8
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => ""} Shulman 15
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
            subtopos is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
            \.
          %p Thus we have found the first step in the process by demanding resolution of the initial opposition. We will denote this by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\flat &amp;\dashv&amp; \sharp &amp; = loc_{\neg \neg}
                  \\\
                  \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \\emptyset &amp;&amp;\dashv&amp; \ast
                  }
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_7 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
              subtopos is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone02"}> Johnstone 02, lemma A4.5.21
            )
          %p
            This means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            is naturally regarded as being the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} ground topos
            of the topos formed by the ambient type system, with the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo ♯
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi Γ
                    %mo ↪
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{H}_{\sharp}
                  \\stackrel{\hookrightarrow}{\stackrel{\stackrel{\Gamma}{\longleftarrow}}{\hookrightarrow}}
                  \\mathbf{H}
          %p
            regarded as the terminal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+points"} global points
            (aka
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+sections"} global sections
          %p
            Therefore we label the resolution of the initial opposition as “ground” for “ground topos”(
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"}> base topos
            ).
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\flat &amp;\dashv&amp; \sharp &amp; = loc_{\not\not}
                  \\\
                  \\vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \\emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast
                  }
                  \\,.
          %h5#cohesive_substance Cohesive substance
          %h6#IntensiveExtensive Quantity
          %p
            This means then that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            is the operation of taking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+points"} global points
            and regarding the collection of them as equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
            structure. Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em moment of pure discreteness
          %p
            This in turn means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
            is the
            %em moment of pure continuity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+topological+space"}> co-discreteness
            ).
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi discreteness
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi continuity
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\stackrel{discreteness}{} &amp; \flat &amp;\dashv&amp; \sharp &amp;= loc_{\not\not}&amp; \stackrel{continuity}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp;
                  }
          %p
            We may hence also say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            is the “point content” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            then this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            of the point content of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em Größe
            of the point content, the
            %em discrete quantity
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi content
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi discreteness
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi continuity
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp;&amp; \flat &amp;&amp; \stackrel{content}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp;&amp; \bot &amp;
                  \\\
                  \\stackrel{discreteness}{} &amp; \flat &amp;\dashv&amp; \sharp &amp; = loc_{\not \not}&amp;  \stackrel{continuity}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp;
                  }
          %p
            The types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that are fully determined by their moment of continuity are those for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \sharp X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            these are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+objects"} concrete objects
            or equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated presheaves
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            which in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            one calls
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+quantities"} intensive quantities
          %p
            Contrary to that, objects which have purely the negative moment of continuity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ♯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\sharp}
            are codomains for “functions” which vanish on points and receive their contribution only from regions that
            %em extend
            beyond a single point. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            these are called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+quantities"} extensive quantities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            in positive degree). This concept of
            %em extension
            is precisely that which gave the name to
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"} Hermann Grassmann
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
            that introduced the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
          %p
            In summary, we have found that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            expresses
            = succeed "," do
              %em quantity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi content
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi discrete
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ♭
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quantity
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi continuous
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi intensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi extensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp;&amp; \flat &amp; &amp;&amp; \stackrel{content}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp;&amp; \bot &amp; &amp;&amp;
                  \\\
                  \\stackrel{discrete}{} &amp; \overline{\flat}/\flat &amp;\stackrel{quantity}{\dashv}&amp; \sharp &amp;  &amp;   &amp; \stackrel{continuous\; (intensive/extensive)}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp;
                  }
          %h6#quality Quality
          %p
            Proceeding, we next demand a second order opposition, def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherOrderOpposition"}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ♭
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ♯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ʃ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;#643; &amp;\dashv&amp; \flat
                  \\\
                  \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\flat &amp;\dashv&amp; \sharp
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            We ask this to have definite negation, def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompleteOpposition"}
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ʃ
                      %mo *
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \ast \simeq \ast
                — the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape"} shape
                of the point is trivial;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♭
                      %mo →
                      %mi ʃ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat \to &amp;#643;
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epi
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-types"} 0-types
                — the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points-to-pieces+transform"} points-to-pieces transform
                is onto.
          %p
            Together these are the axioms of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            as considered in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere07"}> Lawvere 07
            ). (There it is additionally asked that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            preserves binary
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+products"} Cartesian products
          %p
            The intuition is that positing these qualites on a type system makes it, or rather its types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive"} cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
            where points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            are separate but held together by a cohesive attraction which, when the opposing repulsion is removed and only pure
            = succeed "-moment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; X
          %p
            In the more refined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} homotopy toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            modulates
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+infinity-stacks"} locally constant infinity-stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#dcct"}> dcct
            ):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo ♭
                      %msub
                        %mi Grpd
                        %mn ∞
                    %mrow
                      %mi ʃ
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Grpd
                        %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{X \to \flat Grpd_\infty}{&amp;#643; X\to Grpd_\infty}
          %p
            saying that locally constant
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are equivalent to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-permutation+representations"} infinity-permutation representations
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; X
            therefore is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental infinity-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape"} shape
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p Therefore we further add labels as follows.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi shape
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ʃ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi content
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi discrete
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ♭
                            %mo ¯
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quantity
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi continuous
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi intensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi extensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\stackrel{shape}{} &amp;&amp; &amp; &amp;#643; &amp;\stackrel{}{\dashv}&amp; \flat &amp;  &amp; &amp; \stackrel{content}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \bot &amp;\stackrel{}{}&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\stackrel{discrete}{} &amp;\overline{\flat}&amp; / &amp; \flat &amp;\stackrel{quantity}{\dashv}&amp; \sharp &amp; = loc_{\not\not} &amp;   &amp; \stackrel{continuous\; (intensive/extensive)}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp;
                  }
          %p
            This means that in the presence of the further opposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \dashv \flat
            the types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which already had an underlying point content
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            now also have a
            %em shape
            determined by these points sticking together via a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive"} cohesive
            attraction. This is a qualitative aspect of the types in addition to their quantitative moments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♯
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp X
          #ShapeAsAConceptOfSimilarity.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              By remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MomentsAndSimilarity"}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
              determines a concept of similarity of types. This is a well known one: ʃ encodes that two types have the same shape.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              may look like different differential geometric spaces, but
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi similar
                      %mi ʃ
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \, similar_{&amp;#643;} Y)
              holds if they have the same shape.
            %p
              In the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
              given by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"} smooth infinity-groupoids
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SupergeometryIsModel"}
              below, there is for instance the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cylinder
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times (0,1)
              over it, both regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              in the standard way. As such they are not equal (not
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; (S^1) \simeq &amp;#643; (S^1 \times (0,1)) \simeq B \mathbb{Z}
              and hence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi similar
                      %mi ʃ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^1 \, similar_{&amp;#643;} \, (S^1 \times (0,1))
                    \\,.
          %p
            For instance there are now types for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X = \ast
            and yet they may be very different from the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            themselves, hence while quantitatively these do not differ from the point, they must have some quality that distinguishes them from the point. Hence this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %em geometric quality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            this geometric quality is for instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+smooth+manifold"} formal smooth structure
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometric
            structure.
          %p Therefore we write:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi shape
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ʃ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quality
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi content
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi discrete
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ♭
                            %mo ¯
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quantity
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi loc
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi continuous
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi intensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi extensive
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mrow
                            %mi ground
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\stackrel{shape}{} &amp;&amp; &amp; &amp;#643; &amp;\stackrel{quality}{\dashv}&amp; \flat &amp;  &amp; &amp; \stackrel{content}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \bot &amp;\stackrel{}{}&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\stackrel{discrete}{} &amp;\overline{\flat}&amp; / &amp; \flat &amp;\stackrel{quantity}{\dashv}&amp; \sharp &amp; = loc_{\not\not}  &amp;   &amp; \stackrel{continuous\; (intensive/extensive)}{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \vee &amp;\stackrel{ground}{}&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp; \emptyset &amp;\dashv&amp; \ast &amp;
                  }
          %p
            Since with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (&amp;#643; \dashv \sharp)
            we have arrived at an opposition of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\bigcirc \dashv \Box)
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Localization"}
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            is exhibited by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            (def.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Localization"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi loc
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; = loc_{\mathbb{R}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In view of the above interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (&amp;#643;\dashv \flat)
            via higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A1-homotopy+theory"} A1-homotopy theory
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1 = \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as being
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} the continuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            which is the model for the geometric paths that make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;X
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental infinity-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h6#gauge_measure Gauge (Measure)
          %p
            With the concepts given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            and by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (&amp;#643; \dashv \flat)
            thus understood, it remains to find which concept the full unity of unities of opposites
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
