                  }
          %p
            Since these are oppositions of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            is automatic here and not a further axiom.
          %p To see what these new moments mean, observe that now
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \Im X \to &amp;#643; X
          %p
            is a factorization of the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            projection through a finer approximation. Hence in addition to an intrinsic concept of
            %em path
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphism"} 1-morphism
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental infinity-groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
