                  \\,.
          %h6#differential Differential
          %p
            In total, so far these are the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            (
            %a{:href => "#dcct"}> dcct
            ). Using these one may naturally axiomatize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphism"} local diffeomorphism
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalDiffeomorphisms"}
            below),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
            and related concepts.
          %p
            Hence with these moments posited, types now have qualities of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesively
            sticking to each other, the terms in the types now may feel a tighter differential connectedness, we have now a rigidly
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elasticity"} elastic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
          %h5#solid_substance Solid substance
          %h6#Grading Grading
          %p
            Proceed to a new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
            of oppositions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇝
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mi Rh
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℜ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\rightsquigarrow &amp;\dashv&amp; Rh
                  \\\
                  \\vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \\Re &amp;\dashv&amp; \Im
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This gives for each type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a factorization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⇝
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Re X \longrightarrow \stackrel{\rightsquigarrow}{X} \longrightarrow X
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} comparison map
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
          %p
            This hence means now that the purely
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇝
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightsquigarrow
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+object"} reduced type
            and unreduced types, hence they are reduced in some sense, but possibly not properly. Hence there are now two kinds of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇝
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightsquigarrow
          %p
            Hence there is now a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} grading
            on the infinitesimals and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇝
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightsquigarrow
            quotients out everything not in degree 0.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            quality of our formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
            that this encodes so far may hence be thought of as akin to
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kapranov%27s+noncommutative+geometry"} Kapranov's noncommutative geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            which however may have exotic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} infinitesimal thickenings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
          %h6#cyclic_grading Cyclic grading
          %p The moments proceed by a further higher-order opposition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇉
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇝
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇝
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mi Rh
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\rightrightarrows &amp;\dashv&amp; \rightsquigarrow
                  \\\
                  \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\rightsquigarrow &amp;\dashv&amp; Rh
                  }
          %p
            For this to have non-degenerate models one finds that infinitesimals in degree 0 must be allowed to map to products of infinitesimals in non-vanishing degree. This means that the grading is not by a free group, but for instance by a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            = succeed "-grading." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "-grading." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}
          %h6#FormalizationSuperGrading Super
          %p
            We are to require that this level provides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            of the previous oppositions, def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ResolutionOfOpposition"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℑ
                      %mi X
                    %mo ⇝
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\stackrel{\rightsquigarrow}{\Im X} \simeq \Im X
                  \\,.
          %p
            for all types
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mover
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⇉
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Re \stackrel{\rightrightarrows}{U} \simeq \Re U
          %p
            for a set of generators
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          %p
            This says that the reduced part of the even-graded part is the same as the reduced part of the original, hence that odd-grade is removed by reduction, hence that odd-graded moment is nilpotent. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            this is the key consequence of the super-sign rule (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"}> Hermann Grassmann
            , §37 in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_2
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta_1 \theta_2 = -\theta_2 \theta_1
                  \\,,
          %p
            see prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SublationsRealized"}
            below.
          %p
            Hence we think of the above Aufhebungs-condition as further determining the graded function algebras to actually be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
          %p
            By the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ⇝
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\rightsquigarrow}
          %p We indicate this notationally by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} e
                  %mo ≔
                  %mover
                    %mo ⇝
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\e \coloneqq \overline{\rightsquigarrow}
          %p
            We may still further determine this, via def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Localization"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
