          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
            subject to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            given by the above super-grading is yet more rigid than just by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elasticity"} elasticity
            we had before: it exhibits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solidity"} solidity
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p We conclude the process at this point. One may explore it further by continuing it with further resolutions and further oppositions, but for the applications to physics that we consider below the three stages beyond the ground that we have so far turn out to be sufficient.
          %h4#objectivity Objectivity
          %p
            It is a familiar tought in our age, in view of the intimate relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories of physics
            have a natural mathematical formulation, that it is compelling to consider them also just from within mathematics itself. Famous examples include the formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            by
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+classification"}> Wigner classification
            ) and more recently the identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+field+theory"} local topological field theory
            with the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"} symmetric monoidal (infinity,n)-categories
          %p
            Hence while
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            is part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subjective+logic"} subjective logic
            in that it admits the freedom to consider any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            whatsoever, this suggests to identify among these the “objective” mathematical structures which
            %em are
            theories of physics and as such express a more
            %em objective reality
            than random mathematical structures do.
          %h5#FormalizationClassicalMechanics Classical mechanics (Mechanism)
          %p
            We indicate formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            (“the
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a{:href => "#Mechanismus"} mechanism
          %p
            %strong Process
          %p
            Given a logic of concepts as
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ConceptFormalization"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            to a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
            via an intermediate type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            by maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi i
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi o
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{i}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{o}}
                  \\\
                  X_1 &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X_2
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the immediate generalization of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            as we pass from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+0-types"} homotopy 0-types
            to general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            and thereby allow
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            to be replaced by general maps.
          %p
            Now one observe that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            is naturally interpreted as a
            %em process
            \:
          %p
            every instance/term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\colon Y
            may be thought of as a process under which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(y) \colon X_1
            turns into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi o
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o(y) \colon X_2
          %p
            In traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            this is familiar from the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            which serves to encode much of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            %strong Physical law
          %p
            Or rather, classical mechanics is encoded by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondences"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            and hence the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} laws of motion
          %p
            By the discussion there, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondence"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondence
            is itself again just a correspondence, but now in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+and+phase+space+in+physics"} phases
          %p
            A detailed discussion of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            is formalized via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            in the context of a type of phases is at
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Classical+field+theory+via+Cohesive+homotopy+types"} Classical field theory via Cohesive homotopy types
              \.
          %p From the dicussion there one finds a picture of sliced correspondences interpreted as classical mechanics as follows.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %mi space
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mi of
                            %mrow
                              %mi trajectories
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi initial
                                %mrow
                                  %mi values
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
