          %h5#quantum_mechanics_quantumchemism Quantum mechanics ((quantum-)Chemism)
          %p
            Recall from remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AspectsOfTheInitialOpposition"}
            that the initial opposition gave rise also to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"} maybe monad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mi maybe
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\empty} = maybe
          %blockquote
            %p
              The negative of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \id
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %p
              The opposite of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            %p
              The negative of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi maybe
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} maybe
          %p
            While
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi maybe
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} maybe
            is not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PurityForNonIdempotent"}
            we may still ask for the types which are pure with respect to it in that they they are objects in its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg-Moore category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+objects"} pointed types
          %p
            On pointed types the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            structure which is not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} Cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            in the generality of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            here now naturally yield the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory#SecondaryIntegralTransforms"} secondary integral transforms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            are really
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            For details see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantization+via+Linear+homotopy+types"} Quantization via Linear homotopy types
          %h5#boundary_conditions_teleology Boundary conditions (Teleology)
          %p
            In this context a
