                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}^V \to V
          %h4#nature Nature
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of physics, made sufficiently precise in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
            of the theory by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            “is” nature as predicted by this theory.
          %p
            For instance if we considered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            to be the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifolds"} pseudo-Riemannian manifolds
            subject to some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+condition"} energy condition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            for this theory is one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            Above we saw that cohesive (elastic) homotopy type theory contains
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            in its idea, as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            and hence we accordingly find models of nature here.
          %p Recall specifically that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                From prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WZWTermFromCocycle"}
                we have that group cocycles
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 2
                      %mi 𝔾
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{c}\colon \mathbf{B}V \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}^{p+2}\mathbb{G}
                of degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+2
                induce
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+terms"} WZW terms
                in that degree and hence the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-brane"} p-brane
                propagating on the “model
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              %p
                A second cocycle on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-group+extension"} infinity-group extension
                classified by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{c}
                yields a type of
                = succeed "-brane" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde p
                = succeed "-branes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %li
              %p
                This structure is naturally generalized to
                = succeed "-manifolds" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                equipped with definite globalizations of these WZW terms, defining
                = succeed "-branes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                The definite globalization of the WZW term
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi L
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} WZW
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}_{\mathrm{WZW}}
                induces a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Stab
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi L
                        %mi WZW
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stab(\mathbf{L}_{WZW})
                structure on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and the requirement that this be infinitesimally integrable is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+of+a+G-structure"} torsion constraint
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p We now find an externalization of the idea such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                There is a canonical bouquet of higher group cocycles and their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+extensions"} ∞-group extensions
                emanating from the unique 0-truncated purely fermionic type – the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoint"} superpoint
            %li
              %p
                The resulting branes and their intersection laws are those seen in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %li
              %p
                The resulting spacetimes are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspacetimes"} superspacetimes
                as in the relevant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                theories;
            %li
              %p
                The resulting torsion constraints, namely the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+torsion+constraints"} supergravity torsion constraints
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} of motion
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/d%3D4+N%3D1+supergravity"} d=4 N=1 supergravity
                arising in the guise of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
          %p
            This is a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"}> theory of everything
            ” in the sense of modern fundamental physics, which is beeing argued to have viable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} phenomenology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
            for more on this. Even if it turns out that there are no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            in this theory which match quantitative measurements in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            and hence matches faithfully the qualitative features of nature that is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a{:href => "#TheGround"} above
            this is maybe not to be lightly dismissed.
          %h5#Externalization Externalization
          #SupergeometryIsModel.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The cohesive+elastic+solid homotopy type theory
              %a{:href => "#ObjectiveLogic"} above
              has a faithful (i.e. non-degenerate)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} homotopy topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SuperFormalSmooth
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SuperFormalSmooth\infty Grpd
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+formal+smooth+infinity-groupoids"} super formal smooth infinity-groupoids
          %p We now spell out the construction of this model and indicate the proof of this statement.
          #SupergeometricModel.num_defn
            %h6#definition_22 Definition
            %p Write
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
                  for the site of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi InfPoint
                        %mo ≔
                        %msup
                          %mi WAlg
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} InfPoint \coloneqq WAlg^{op}
                  for the category of first-order
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
                  (i.e. the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+duality"} formal duals
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebras"} commutative algebras
                  over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                  of the form
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\oplus V
                  with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  a finite-dimensional square-0
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideal"} nilpotent ideal
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SuperPoint
                        %mo ≔
                        %msubsup
                          %mi WAlg
                          %mi super
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SuperPoint \coloneqq WAlg_{super}^{op}
                  for the category of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duals"} formal duals
                  to commutative
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
                  which are super-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebras"}> Weil algebras
                  \.
            %p
              There are then “semidirect product” sites
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo ⋊
                    %mi InfinPoint
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp \rtimes InfinPoint
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo ⋊
                    %mi SuperPoint
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp \rtimes SuperPoint
              (whose objects are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+products"} Cartesian products
              of the given form inside
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+supergeometry"} synthetic differential supergeometry
              and whose morphisms are all morphisms in that context (not just the product morphisms)).
            %p Set then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Smooth
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Smooth \infty Grpd \coloneqq Sh_\infty(CartSp)
            %p
              for the collection of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FormalSmooth
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo ⋊
                    %mi InfPoint
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    FormalSmooth\infty Grpd \coloneqq Sh_\infty(CartSp \rtimes InfPoint)
            %p
              for the collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} formal smooth ∞-groupoids
