                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Ansichsein &amp;\stackrel{Etwas}{\dashv}&amp; Sein-fuer-Anderes
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This will repeat in the Wesenslogik with
            %em Etwas
            replaced by
            = succeed "," do
              %em Ding
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#1048"} §1048
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th
                %th moment
                %th unity
                %th comoment
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Seinslogik
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Sein
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Ansichseyn
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Etwas
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Sein-fuer-Anderes
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Wesenslogik
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Existenz
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Ding-an-sich
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Ding
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} äußerliche Existenz
          %p
            Notice that there in the discussion of
            %em Ding
            is a comment hidden that concerns the
            %em Etwas
            here:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "#1056"} §1056
              Die Qualität ist die unmittelbare Bestimmtheit des Etwas; das Negative selbst, wodurch das Seyn Etwas ist.
            %p §1056 Quality is the immediate determinateness of something, the negative itself through which being is something.
          %p
            This says that the the “immediate determinateness” of the adjunction in
            %a{:href => "#221"} §221
            is the adjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            that we identify with
            = succeed "." do
              %em quality
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Process"} Process
          %blockquote
            %p §222 Being and nothing in their unity, which is determinate being
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            discussed around
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#182"} §182
            %a{:href => "#183"} §183
          %blockquote
            %p#226 §226 Dieß führt zu einer weitern Bestimmung. Ansichseyn und Seyn-für-Anderes sind zunächst verschieden; aber daß Etwas dasselbe, was es an sich ist, auch an ihm hat, und umgekehrt, was es als Seyn-für-Anderes ist, auch an sich ist,—dieß ist die Identität des Ansichseyns und Seyns-für-Anderes, nach der Bestimmung, daß das Etwas selbst ein und dasselbe beider Momente ist, sie also ungetrennt in ihm sind.—Es ergiebt sich formell diese Identität schon in der Sphäre des Daseyns, aber ausdrücklicher in der Betrachtung des Wesens und dann des Verhältnisses der Innerlichkeit und Äußerlichkeit, und am bestimmtesten in der Betrachtung der Idee, als der Einheit des Begriffs und der Wirklichkeit.
            %p §226 This leads to a further determination. Being-in-itself and being-for-other are, in the first instance, distinct; but that something also has within it the same character that it is in itself, and, conversely, that what it is as being-for-other it also is in itself-this is the identity of being-in-itself and being-for-other, in accordance with the determination that the something itself is one and the same something of both moments, which, therefore, are undividedly present in it. This identity is already formally given in the sphere of determinate being, but more expressly in the consideration of essence and of the relation of inner and outer, and most precisely in the consideration of the Idea as the unity of the Notion and actuality.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Sphäre
                %th
                %th Moment
                %th Einheit
                %th Komoment
                %th
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Seinslogik
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Dasein
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Ansichsein
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Etwas
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Sein-für-Anderes
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a{:href => "#226"} §226
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Wesenslogik
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Existenz
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Inneres / Ding-An-Sich
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Ding
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Äußeres
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a{:href => "#1048c"}> §1048c
                  ,
                  %a{:href => "#1149"} §1149
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Begriffslogik
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Begriff
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Idee
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Wirklichkeit
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a{:href => "#1636"} §1636
          %p
            For the following comment on the thing-in-itself, see also (
            %a{:href => "#EL124"}> EL§124
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "EL&#167;124Zusatz"} #EL124Zusatz
          %blockquote
            %p#227 §227 Es kann bemerkt werden, daß sich hier der Sinn des Dings-an-sich ergibt, das eine sehr einfache Abstraktion ist, aber eine Zeitlang eine sehr wichtige Bestimmung, gleichsam etwas Vornehmes, so wie der Satz, daß wir nicht wissen, was die Dinge an sich sind, eine vielgeltende Weisheit war. - Die Dinge heißen an-sich, insofern von allem Sein-für-Anderes abstrahiert wird, das heißt überhaupt, insofern sie ohne alle Bestimmung, als Nichtse gedacht werden. In diesem Sinn kann man freilich nicht wissen, was das Ding an-sich ist. Denn die Frage Was? verlangt, daß Bestimmungen angegeben werden; indem aber die Dinge, von denen sie anzugeben verlangt würde, zugleich Dinge-an-sich sein sollen, das heißt eben ohne Bestimmung, so ist in die Frage gedankenloserweise die Unmöglichkeit der Beantwortung gelegt, oder man macht nur eine widersinnige Antwort. - Das Ding-an-sich ist dasselbe, was jenes Absolute, von dem man nichts weiß, als daß Alles eins in ihm ist. Man weiß daher sehr wohl, was an diesen Dingen-an-sich ist; sie sind als solche nichts als wahrheitslose, leere Abstraktionen. Was aber das Ding-an-sich in Wahrheit ist, was wahrhaft an sich ist, davon ist die Logik die Darstellung, wobei aber unter Ansich etwas Besseres als die Abstraktion verstanden wird, nämlich was etwas in seinem Begriffe ist; dieser aber ist konkret in sich, als Begriff überhaupt begreiflich und als bestimmt und Zusammenhang seiner Bestimmungen in sich erkennbar.
            %p §227 It may be observed that the meaning of the thing-in-itself is here revealed; it is a very simple abstraction but for some while it counted as a very important determination, something superior, as it were, just as the proposition that we do not know what things are in themselves ranked as a profound piece of wisdom. Things are called ‘in themselves’ in so far as abstraction is made from all being-for-other, which means simply, in so far as they are thought devoid of all determination, as nothings. In this sense, it is of course impossible to know what the thing-in-itself is. For the question: what? Demands that determinations be assigned; but since the things of which they are to be assigned are at the same time supposed to be things in-themselves, which means, in effects to,- be without any determination, the question is thoughtlessly made impossible to answer, or else only an absurd answer is given. The thing-in-itself is the same as that absolute of which we know nothing except that in it all is one. What is in these things-in-themselves, therefore, we know quite well; they are as such nothing but truthless, empty abstractions. What, however, the thing-in-itself is in truth, what truly is in itself, of this logic is the exposition, in which however something better than an abstraction is understood by ‘in-itself’, namely, what something is in its Notion; but the Notion is concrete within itself, is comprehensible simply as Notion, and as determined within itself and the connected whole of its determinations, is cognisable.
          %h5#c_die_unendlichkeit C. Die Unendlichkeit
          %h6#a_das_unendliche_berhaupt a. Das Unendliche Überhaupt
          %h6#b_wechselwirkung_des_endlichen_und_unendlichen b. Wechselwirkung des Endlichen und Unendlichen
          %h6#c_die_affirmative_unendlichkeit c. Die affirmative Unendlichkeit
          %blockquote
            %p#304 §304 In Beziehung auf Realität und Idealität wird aber der Gegensatz des Endlichen und Unendlichen so gefaßt, daß das Endliche für das Reale gilt, das Unendliche aber für das Ideelle gilt; wie auch weiterhin der Begriff als ein Ideelles und zwar als ein nur Ideelles, das Daseyn überhaupt dagegen als das Reale betrachtet wird.
          %blockquote
            %p With reference to reality and ideality, however, the opposition of finite and infinite is grasped in such a manner that the finite ranks as the real but the infinite as the ‘ideal’ [das Ideelle]; in the same way that further on the Notion, too, is regarded as an ‘ideal’, that is, as a mere ‘ideal’, in contrast to determinate being as such which is regarded as the real.
          %p
            Notice here from
            %em
              %a{:href => "#DerQuantitativeUnendlicheProgress"} Der quantitative unendliche Progress
            and
            %a{:href => "#530"} §530
            that “infinite” refers much to the
            %em infinite progression
            that in mathematics is referred to as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/series"} series
            %em ideell
            ) about them is that they need not converge to any finite value, but be regarded as sequences — such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            %a{:href => "#502"} §502
            – then
            %a{:href => "#304"} §304
            gives that “the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            ranks as the ideal”, whereas “the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+object"} reduced
            ranks as the real”. See also the discussion below
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#305"} §305
          %p
            This way we may think of the “ideal” here as related to the idealization involved in the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            = succeed "," do
              %em finite
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            is the operation that makes all infinitesimal vanish, i.e. it expresses precisely the “vanishing of infinitesimals” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#174"} §174
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            are precisely those without infinitesimal extension. It is perfectly plausible to think of these as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em real types
          %p
            Moreover, the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-modal+types"} anti-modal types
            are indeed precisely those that consist entirely only of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#304"} §304
          %p
            We discuss below
            %a{:href => "#305"} §305
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            that
            = succeed "-participates" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            that this may be thought to express.
          %h6#der_bergang Der Übergang
          %blockquote
            %p#305
              §305 Die Idealität kann die Qualität der Unendlichkeit genannt werden; aber sie ist wesentlich der Proceß des Werdens und damit ein Übergang, wie des Werdens in Daseyn, der nun anzugeben ist. Als
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufheben
              der Endlichkeit, d. i. der Endlichkeit als solcher und ebenso sehr der ihr nur gegenüberstehenden, nur negativen Unendlichkeit ist diese Rückkehr in sich, Beziehung auf sich selbst, Seyn. Da in diesem Seyn Negation ist, ist es Daseyn, aber da sie ferner wesentlich Negation der Negation, die sich auf sich beziehende Negation ist, ist sie das Daseyn, welches Fürsichseyn genannt wird.
            %p
              §305 Ideality can be called the
              %em quality
              of infinity; but it is essentially the process of
              = succeed "," do
                %em becoming
              = succeed "," do
                %em self-relation
              = succeed "." do
                %em being
              = succeed "," do
                %em determinate
              = succeed "." do
                %em being-for-self
          %p
            If here we take the “infinite” and the “infinitesimal” to go together – as in
            %a{:href => "#502"} §502
            – then the above would give also that
            = succeed "." do
              %em ideality is the quality of the infinitesimal
          %p
            Now we have from
            %a{:href => "#699"} §699
            that
            = succeed "," do
              %em quality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+of+opposites"} duality of opposites
            formalized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi quality
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi ʃ
                          %mo ⊣
                          %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  quality \colon &amp;#643; \dashv \flat
                  }
          %p
            This indeed has a direct infinitesimal analog, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℑ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Im
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+flat+modality"} infinitesimal flat modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi &amp;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
          %p
            This clearly suggests to translate “ideality is the quality of the infinite” in
            %a{:href => "#305"} §305
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            which is expressed by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Im \dashv \&amp;
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mover
                              %mo ⊣
                              %mrow
                                %mi inf
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mi quality
                            %mi ideality
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ʃ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quality
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp;  \Im &amp;\stackrel{ideality}{\stackrel{inf\, quality}{\dashv}}&amp; \&amp;
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;  &amp;#643; &amp;\stackrel{quality}{\dashv}&amp; \flat
                  }
          %p
            as part of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Process"} Proceß
            %a{:href => "#322"} §322
            we see what the moments here are to be called:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mtext being-for-self
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mover
                              %mo ⊣
                              %mrow
                                %mi inf
                                %mo .
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mi quality
                            %mrow
                              %mi ideality
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                        %mtd
                          %mtext being-for-one
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi attraction
                        %mtd
                          %mi ʃ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi quality
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                        %mtd
                          %mi repulsion
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; \text{being-for-self} &amp;  \Im &amp;\stackrel{ideality /}{\stackrel{inf.\, quality}{\dashv}}&amp; \&amp;  &amp; \text{being-for-one}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \vee &amp;&amp; \vee
                  \\\
                  \&amp; attraction &amp;  &amp;#643; &amp;\stackrel{quality}{\dashv}&amp; \flat &amp; repulsion
                  }
          %p
            In fact, this provides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            for quality, as indicated, which we may read as the Aufhebung of the finite as it passes into the infinite (which we read as expressed via the infinitesimal).
          %p
            Moreover, this extends to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi ℑ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi &amp;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re \dashv \Im \dashv \&amp;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            (from the discussion below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#304"} §304
          %p This hence gives a second order unity of opposites
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℜ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi ideality
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mrow
                                %mrow
                                  %mi inf
                                  %mo .
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mi quality
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \Re &amp;\dashv&amp; \Im
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \Im &amp;\stackrel{{ideality/ \atop {inf.\,quality}}}{\dashv}&amp; \&amp;
                  }
          %p
            and hence exibits a dual moment of ideality, which, by the discussion below
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#304"} §304
          %p
            Now by
            %a{:href => "#324"} §324
            the opposite moment of ideality is indeed supposed to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em reality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℜ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi reality
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi ideality
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mrow
                                %mrow
                                  %mi inf
                                  %mo .
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mi quality
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \Re &amp;\stackrel{reality}{\dashv}&amp; \Im
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \Im &amp;\stackrel{{ideality/ \atop {inf.\,quality}}}{\dashv}&amp; \&amp;
                  }
          %p
            and interpret not just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            alone as being about reality, but as being just one moment of it, the other moment being expresed by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℑ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Im
          %p
            This happens to make good sense: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
