          %h5#a_das_frsichsein_als_solches__beingforself_as_such A. Das Fürsichsein als solches / Being-for-self as such
          %h6#a_dasein_und_frsichsein__determinate_being_and_beingforself a. Dasein und Fürsichsein / Determinate being and Being-for-self
          %blockquote
            %p#321 §321 Das Fürsichseyn ist, wie schon erinnert ist, die in das einfache Seyn zusammengesunkene Unendlichkeit; es ist Daseyn, insofern die negative Natur der Unendlichkeit, welche Negation der Negation ist, in der nunmehr gesetzten Form der Unmittelbarkeit des Seyns, nur als Negation überhaupt, als einfache qualitative Bestimmtheit ist.
          %blockquote
            %p §321 But being, which in such determinateness is determinate being, is also at once distinct from being-for-self, which is only being-for-self in so far as its determinateness is the infinite one above-mentioned; nevertheless, determinate being is at the same time also a moment of being-for-self; for this latter, of course, also contains being charged with negation. Thus the determinateness which in determinate being as such is an other, and a being-for-other, is bent back into the infinite unity of being-for-self, and the moment of determinate being is present in being-for-self as a being-for-one.
          %h6#b_seynfreines__beingforone b. Seyn-für-eines / Being-for-one
          %blockquote
            %p#322 §322 Seyn-für-eines – Dieß Moment drückt aus, wie das Endliche in seiner Einheit mit dem Unendlichen oder als Ideelles ist.
            %p Für-sich-seyn und Für-Eines-seyn sind also nicht verschiedene Bedeutungen der Idealität, sondern sind wesentliche, untrennbare Momente derselben.
            %p §322 Being-for-one – This moment expresses the manner in which the finite is present in its unity with the infinite, or is an ideal being [Ideelles].
            %p §322 To be ‘for self’ and to be ‘for one’ are therefore not different meanings of ideality, but are essential, inseparable moments of it.
          %p
            By the discussion below
            %a{:href => "#305"} §305
            the inclusion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modal
            types into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+flat+modality"} infinitesimal flat modality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∨
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\&amp;
                  \\\
                  \\vee
                  \\\
                  \\flat
                  }
          %p
            reflects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            which is the passage from the finite to the infinitesimal.
          %p
            So we may pronounce, in the
            %a{:href => "#Process"} Proceß
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+flat+modality"} infinitesimal flat modality
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %em Seyn-fuer-eines
            = succeed "." do
              %em being-for-one
          %p
            Below
            %a{:href => "#305"} §305
            we find the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            which plausibly captures the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            called ideality, and hence by
            %a{:href => "#322"} §322
            what its moments are to be called.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Fuersichsein
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℑ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⊣
                            %mi Idealitaet
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                        %mtd
                          %mi Fuereinssein
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Fuersichsein &amp; \Im &amp;\stackrel{Idealitaet}{\dashv}&amp; \&amp;  &amp; Fuereinssein
                  }
          %p
            See also the discussion below
            %a{:href => "#348"} §348
          %h6#RemarkOnRealityAsOppositeToDuality Anmerkung
          %blockquote
            %p#324 §324 Die Idealität kommt zunächst den aufgehobenen Bestimmungen zu, als unterschieden von dem, worin sie aufgehoben sind, das dagegen als das Reelle genommen werden kann. So aber ist das Ideelle wieder eins der Momente und das Reale das andere;
            %p §324 But thus the ideal is again one of the moments, and the real the other;
          %p
            Hence we have another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ideality
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi reality
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ideality \dashv reality
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#EinsUndVieles"} The One and the Many
          %p
            It seems that in
            %em Science of Logic
            there is no
            %em name
            given to this unity. But in the
            %a{:href => "#EPSBOI"} shorter logic
            there is:
          %blockquote
            %p#EL214 EL§214 Die Idee kann als die Vernunft, (dies ist die eigentliche philosophische Bedeutung der Vernunft), ferner als das Subjekt-Objekt, als die Einheit des Ideellen und Reellen, des Endlichen und Unendlichen, der Seele und des Leibs, als die Möglichkeit, die ihre Wirklichkeit an ihr selbst hat, als das, dessen Natur nur als existierend begriffen werden kann, u.s.f gefaßt werden; weil in ihr alle Verhältnisse des Verstandes, aber in ihrer unendlichen Rückkher, und Identität in sich enthalten sind.
            %p EL§214 The Idea may be described in many ways. It may be called reason; (and this is the proper philosophical signification of reason); subject-object; the unity of the ideal and the real, of the finite and the infinite, of soul and body; the possibility which has its actuality in its own self; that of which the nature can be thought only as existent, etc.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} real
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} ideal
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} finite
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} infinitesimal
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} body
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} soul
          %p
            (Regarding “soul and body” see also the comments at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheMonadOfLeibniz"} The monad of Leibniz
          %p
            But see below
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheIdea"} The idea
            %em notion
            and the real. See the discussion below
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#1638"} §1683
          %h6#c_eins c. Eins
          %blockquote
            %p §328 Being-for-self is the simple unity of itself and its moment, being-for-one.
            %p §329 The moments which constitute the Notion of the one as a being-for-self fall asunder in the development. They are: (1) negation in general, (2) two negations, (3) two that are therefore the same, (4) sheer opposites, (5) self-relation, identity as such, (6) relation which is negative and yet to its own self.
          %p
            If we translate “moment” as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            then here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+modality"} double negation modality
            comes to mind.
          %p
            Notice that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+modality"} double negation modality
          %h5#EinsUndVieles B. Eins und Vieles. / The One and the Many
          %blockquote
            %p Die Idealität des Fürsichseyns als Totalität schlägt so fürs erste in die Realität um, und zwar in die festeste, abstrakteste, als Eins.
          %h6#a_das_eins_an_ihm_selbst a. Das Eins an ihm selbst
          %h6#b_das_eins_und_das_leere__the_one_and_the_void b. Das Eins und das Leere / The One and the Void
          %blockquote
            %p §335 The one is the void as the abstract relation of the negation to itself.
          %h6#Atomism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;
            Remark: Atomism
          %blockquote
            %p §337 The one in this form of determinate being is the stage of the category which made its appearance with the ancients as the atomistic principle, according to which the essence of things is the atom and the void.
          %p
            Eins:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom"} atom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
          %h6#c_viele_eins_repulsion__many_ones_repulsion c. Viele Eins. Repulsion. / Many ones. Repulsion.
          %blockquote
            %p#340 §340 The one and the void constitute the first stage of the determinate being of being-for-self. Each of these moments has negation for its determination and is at the same time posited as a determinate being; according to the former determination the one and the void are the relation of negation to negation as of an other to its other: the one is negation in the determination of being, and the void is negation in the determination of non-being.
          %p
            Das Eins (the One):
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
          %p
            Das Leere (the void):
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            (
            %em leere Menge
            \!)
          %p
            Negation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\not X) \coloneqq (X \to \emptyset)
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \simeq \not \emptyset
            \.
          %blockquote
            %p#342 §342 the one repels itself from itself. The negative relation of the one to itself is repulsion.
            %p §343 This repulsion as thus the positing of many ones but through the one itself, is the one’s own coming-forth-from-itself but to such outside it as are themselves only ones. This is repulsion according to its Notion, repulsion in itself. The second repulsion is different from it, it is what is immediately suggested to external reflection: repulsion not as the generation of ones, but only as the mutual repelling of ones presupposed as already present.
          %p
            To see a formalization of “the one repels itself from itself”, suppose we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            but without the assumption that it preserves finite product types. (This is what the term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}> shape
            ” really refers to).
          %p
            Then given just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            to be formed (since necessarily
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \emptyset \simeq \emptyset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; {}_{\ast}
          %p
            Below we see that this, being a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+type"} discrete type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \ast
            do not attract/cohese, they are different and repel.
          %p
            At the same time, being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+type"} discrete type
            it is necessarily a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            of copies of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "limit+in+a+quasi-category#Tensoring"} here
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟶
                    %mi I
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; {}_{\ast} \simeq \underset{\longrightarrow}{\lim}_I \ast
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            that the colimit is over is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mi ʃ
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = &amp;#643; {}_{\ast}
            itself.
          %p
            For a similar argument see Lawvere’s
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#LawvereLauterEinsen"}> Cohesive toposes and Cantor’s Lauter Einsen
                )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            “all elements of a set are indistinguishable and yet distinct” is captured by the fact that both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            as well as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp X
            have the same image under
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
          %h6#TheMonadOfLeibniz
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;
            Remark: The Monad of Leibniz
          %blockquote
            %p#348
              §348 We have previously referred to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Leibnizian
              idealism. We may add here that this idealism which started from the ideating
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28disambiguation%29"} monad
            %p The atomistic philosophy does not possess the Notion of ideality; it does not grasp the one as an ideal being, that is, as containing within itself the two moments of being-forself and being-for-it, but only as a simple, dry, real being-for-self.
            %p It does, however, go beyond mere indifferent plurality; the atoms become further determined in regard to one another even though, strictly speaking, this involves an inconsistency; whereas, on the contrary, in that indifferent independence of the monads, plurality remains as a fixed fundamental determination, so that the connection between them falls only in the monad of monads, or in the philosopher who contemplates them.
          %p
            To summarize, in
            %a{:href => "#322"} §322
            we get a clear prescription:
          %blockquote
            %p To be ‘for self’ and to be ‘for one’ are therefore not different meanings of ideality, but are essential, inseparable moments of it.
          %p
            So we are to find an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            that expresses
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ideality
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi BeingForSelf
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi BeingForOne
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ideality \;\colon\; BeingForSelf \dashv BeingForOne
          %p (or possibly the other way around).
          %p The complaint about Leibniz in §348, makes pretty clear what this is about:
          %blockquote
            %p The atomistic philosophy does not possess the Notion of ideality; it does not grasp the one as an ideal being, that is, as containing within itself the two moments of being-forself and being-for-it, but only as a simple, dry, real being-for-self.
          %p
            Here “atoms” really refers to the decomposition of the continuum into points (atoms of space, as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+nonstandard+analysis"} monad in nonstandard analysis
          %blockquote
            %p The one in this form of determinate being is the stage of the category which made its appearance with the ancients as the atomistic principle, according to which the essence of things is the atom and the void.
          %p
            But “The one” (The unit) with its repulsion of many we claimed before is well modeled by what
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            produces, the underlying points, the atoms of space.
          %p
            So in conclusion the statement here is that it is a defect of both the ancients as well as of Leibniz to consider atoms/monads/points which have no way to look outside of themselves into interaction with others, that instead one needs to characterized atoms/monads/points by the above adjoint modality which expresses
            = succeed "." do
              %em Ideality
          %p
            In conclusion,
            %em Eins
            (“The One”/“The Unit”) is a notion of atom which is similar to what the ancients and Leibniz called atom/monad, only that it improves on that by keeping an additional “moment” which the ancients and Leibniz forgot to retain.
          %p
            Now in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            %em
              %a{:href => "#LawvereMotion"} Toposes of Laws of Motion
            “atom” is proposed to refer to, essentially,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
          %p
            So possibly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            captures some of this well.
          %p
            Here is a cartoon of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
            with its infinitesimal antennas reaching out to test what’s going on around
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo −
                          %mo •
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \-- \bullet --
                  }
          %p
            and here is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+object"} reduced point
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\bullet
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            one says “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soul"}> soul
            ” for these “antennas” and “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/body"}> body
            ” for what remains. (This happens to fit well with
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#EL214"} EL§214
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ideality
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi BeingForSelf
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi BeingForOne
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ideality \;\colon\; BeingForSelf \dashv BeingForOne
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
          %h5#c_repulsion_und_attraktion C. Repulsion und Attraktion
          %h6#a_ausschlien_des_eins a. Ausschlißen des Eins.
          %p#UnityOfTheOneAndTheMany
            %strong Remark: The unity of the One and the Many
          %blockquote
            %p#357
              §357 It is an ancient proposition that the one is many and especially that the many are one. We may repeat here the observation that the truth of the one and the many expressed in propositions appears in an inappropriate form, that this truth is to be grasped and expressed only as a becoming, as a process, a repulsion and attraction-not as being, which in a proposition has the character of a stable unity. We have already mentioned and recalled the dialectic of Plato in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Parmenides"} Parmenides
              concerning the derivation of the many from the one, namely, from the proposition: the one is. The inner dialectic of the Notion has been stated; it is easiest to grasp the dialectic of the proposition, that the many are one, as an external reflection; and it may properly be grasped externally here inasmuch as the object too, the many, are mutually external. It directly follows from this comparison of the many with one another that any one is determined simply like any other one; each is a one, each is one of the many, is by excluding the others — so that they are absolutely the same, there is present one and only one determination. This is the fact, and all that has to be done is to grasp this simple fact. The only reason why the understanding stubbornly refuses to do so is that it has also in mind, and indeed rightly so, the difference; but the existence of this difference is just as little excluded because of the said fact, as is the certain existence of the said fact in spite of the difference. One could, as it were, comfort understanding for the naive manner in which it grasps the fact of the difference, by assuring it that the difference will
          %h6#b_das_eine_eins_der_attraktion b. Das eine Eins der Attraktion
          %h6#RelationBetweenRepulsionAndAttraction c. Die Beziehung der Repulsion und der Attraktion
          %blockquote
            %p#361 §361 The difference of the one and the many is now determined as the difference of their relation to one another, with each other, a relation which splits into two, repulsion and attraction, each of which is at first independent of the other and stands apart from it, the two nevertheless being essentially connected with each other. Their as yet indeterminate unity is to be more precisely ascertained
          %p
            So we are looking now for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi attraction
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi repulsion
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  attraction \dashv repulsion
                  
                  \\,.
          %p
            The natural choice is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ʃ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \dashv \flat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \mathbb{R}^n \simeq \ast
            is a One into which all the points of the space have collapsed, whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat \mathbb{R}^n
            is the many Ones out of which this space consist.
          %blockquote
            %p#369 §369 Die Repulsion daseyender Eins ist die Selbsterhaltung des Eins durch die gegenseitige Abhaltung der andern, so daß 1) die anderen Eins an ihm negirt werden, dieß ist die Seite seines Daseyns oder seines Seyns-für-Anderes; diese ist aber somit Attraktion, als die Idealität der Eins;—und daß 2) das Eins an sich sey, ohne die Beziehung auf die andere; aber nicht nur ist das Ansich überhaupt längst in das Fürsichseyn übergegangen, sondern an sich, seiner Bestimmung nach, ist das Eins jenes Werden zu Vielen.—Die Attraktion daseyender Eins ist die Idealität derselben, und das Setzen des Eins, worin sie somit als Negiren und Hervorbringen des Eins sich selbst aufhebt, als Setzen des Eins das Negative ihrer selbst an ihr, Repulsion ist.
            %p §369 The repulsion of the determinately existent ones is the self-preservation of the one through the mutual repulsion of the others, so that (1) the other ones are negated in it-this is the side of its determinate being or of its being-for-other; but this is thus attraction as the ideality of the ones; and (2) the one is in itself, without relation to the others; but not only has being-in-itself as such long since passed over into being-for-self, but the one in itself, by its determination, is the aforesaid becoming of many ones. The attraction of the determinately existent ones is their ideality and the positing of the one, in which, accordingly, attraction as a negating and a generating of the one sublates itself, and as a positing of the one is in its own self the negative of itself, repulsion.
          %blockquote
            %p §370 Damit ist die Entwickelung des Fürsichseyns vollendet und zu ihrem Resultate gekommenen.
            %p §370 With this, the development of being-for-self is completed and has reached its conclusion.
            %p#372
              §372 This unity is, therefore,
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              determinate being
          %p
            Since
            = succeed "," do
              %em determinate being
            %em Dasein
            and
            %em Fürsichsein
            both are qualitative being/are quality (
            %a{:href => "#188"}> §188
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#318"} §318
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#321"} §321
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            from
            %a{:href => "#361"} §361
            should be
            %em quality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi quality
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi attraction
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi repulsion
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  quality \;\colon\; attraction \dashv repulsion
          %p
            By
