          %h5#b_kontinuirliche_und_diskrete_gre B. Kontinuirliche und diskrete Größe.
          %h6#on_the_continuum On the continuum
          %blockquote
            %p §400 Mathematics, on the other hand, rejects a metaphysics which would make time consist of points of time; space in general — or in the first place the line — consist of points of space; the plane, of lines; and total space of planes. It allows no validity to such discontinuous ones. Even though, for instance, in determining the magnitude of a plane, it represents it as the sum of infinitely many lines, this discreteness counts only as a momentary representation, and the sublation of the discreteness is already implied in the infinite plurality of the lines, since the space which they are supposed to constitute is after all bounded.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
          %blockquote
            %p Diese Antinomie besteht allein, darin daß die Diskretion eben so sehr als die Kontinuität behauptet werden muß. Die einseitige Behauptung der Diskretion giebt das unendliche oder absolute Getheiltseyn, somit ein Untheilbares zum Princip; die einseitige Behauptung der Kontinuität dagegen die unendliche Theilbarkeit.
          %h6#on_space_time_matter On space, time, matter
          %blockquote
            %p §432 Space, time, matter, and so forth are continuous magnitudes
          %h5#c_begrenzung_der_quantitt C. Begrenzung der Quantität
          %h4#second_chapter_quantum Second chapter. Quantum
          %blockquote
            %p §437 Quantum, which to begin with is quantity with a determinateness or limit in general is, in its complete determinateness, number. Quantum differentiates itself secondly, into (a) extensive quantum, in which the limit is a limitation of the determinately existent plurality; and (b) intensive quantum or degree, the determinate being having made the transition into being-for-self. Intensive quantum as both for itself and at the same time immediately outside itself — since it is an indifferent limit — has its determinateness in an other. As this manifest contradiction of being determined simply within itself yet having its determinateness outside it, pointing outside itself for it, quantum posited as being in its own self external to itself, passes over thirdly, into quantitative infinity.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+and+intensive+quantity"} extensive and intensive quantity
          %h5#a_die_zahl A. Die Zahl
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number+object"} natural number object
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete object
          %blockquote
            %p §441 Quantum completely posited in these determinations is number. The complete positedness lies in the existence of the limit as a plurality and so in its distinction from the unity. Consequently, number appears as a discrete magnitude, but in the unity it equally possesses continuity.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number+object"} real number object
          %h5#ExtensiveAndIntensiveQuantity B. Extensives und Intensives Quantum
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+and+intensive+quantity"} extensive and intensive quantity
          %p
            In modern
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+quantity"} extensive quantity
                is one expressed by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
                in positve degree (for instance a mass density 3-form on Euclidean 3-space) – this is close to the “extension” of differential forms in
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Grassmann
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Grassmann"} ?
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
            %li
              %p
                an
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+quantity"} intensive quantity
                is one expressed by a differential form of degree 0, namely by a function (for instance a temperator function on that Euclidean 3-space).
          %p
            The existence and difference between these two concepts is neatly encoded by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
          %p
            an intensive quantity object such as the smooth sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            of real numbers is characterized by being a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+object"} concrete object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit
            of the sharp modality is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ↪
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \hookrightarrow \sharp \mathbb{R}
            \.
          %p
            On the other hand extensive quantities such as given by the sheaves
