            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            constitutes the field of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            that we are using for formalization here (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"}> modal type theory
            ), namely by identifying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility"} necessity and possibility
            monads (as also discussed there) as one of the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pairs"} adjoint pairs
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            of a type of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"}> possible worlds
            ”. Then we discuss that the
            %em other
            adjoint pair induced by this adjoint triple indeed captures randomness, in that its monad may be thought of as producing space of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variables"} random variables
          %p
