                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [W,V]
            %em are
            precisely the kind of random variables in the physical universe that probability theory is designed to model.
          %p
            In this vein, the Haskell-introduction
            %a{:href => "function+monad#Verdier14"} Verdier 14
            says:
          %blockquote
            %p The intuition behind the Reader monad, for a mathematician, is perhaps stochastic variables. A stochastic variable is a function from a probability space to some other space. So we see a stochastic variable as a monadic value.
          %p
            In a more technical style the very same point may be found made in (
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/function+monad#TorontoMcCarthy10b"}> Toronto-McCarthy 10b, slide 24
            ) and then
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "function+monad#TorontoMcCarthy10b"} Toronto-McCarthy 10b, slide 35
          %blockquote
