          %h5#PossibilityAndNecessity A Zufälligkeit oder formelle Wirklichkeit, Möglichkeit und Nothwendigkeit/ Randomness or Formal Actuality, Possibility and Necessity
          %blockquote
            %p#1192 §1192 1. Die Wirklichikeit ist formell, insofern sie als erste Wirklichkeit nur unmittelbare, unreflektirte Wirklichkeit, somit nur in dieser Formbestimmung, aber nicht als Totalität der Form ist. Sie ist so weiter nichts als ein Seyn oder Existenz überhaupt. Aber weil sie wesentlich nicht bloße unmittelbare Existenz, sondern, als Formeinheit des Ansichseyns oder der Innerlichkeit, und der Äußerlichkeit ist, so enthält sie unmittelbar das Ansichseyn oder die Möglichkeit. Was wirklich ist, ist möglich.
            %p §1192 1. Actuality is formal in so far as, being primary actuality, it is only immediate, unreflected actuality, and hence is only in this form-determination but not as the totality of form. As such it is nothing more than a being or Existence in general. But because it is essentially not a mere immediate Existence but exists as form-unity of being-within-self or inwardness and outwardness, it immediately contains the in-itself or possibility. What is actual is possible.
          %p
            see below
            %a{:href => "#1191"} §1191
          %h5#b_relative_nothwendigkeit_oder_reale_wirklichkeit_mglichkeit_und_nothwendigkeit__relative_necessity_or_real_actuality_possibility_and_necessity B. Relative Nothwendigkeit oder reale Wirklichkeit, Möglichkeit und Nothwendigkeit / Relative Necessity, or Real Actuality, Possibility and Necessity
          %p
            see below
            %a{:href => "#1191"} §1191
          %h5#c_absolute_nothwendigkeit__absolute_necessity C. Absolute Nothwendigkeit / Absolute Necessity
          %h4#das_absolute_verhltni Das absolute Verhältniß
          %blockquote
            %p#1234 §1234 Dieß Verhältniß in seinem unmittelbaren Begriff ist das Verhältniß der Substanz und der Accidenzen, das unmittelbare Verschwinden und Werden des absoluten Scheines in sich selbst. Indem die Substanz sich zum Fürsichseyn gegen ein Anderes bestimmt, oder das absolute Verhältniß als reales, ist das Verhältniß der Kausalität. Endlich indem dieses als sich auf sich Beziehendes in Wechselwirkung übergeht, so ist damit das absolute Verhältniß nach den Bestimmungen, welche es enthält, auch gesetzt; diese gesetzte Einheit seiner in seinen Bestimmungen, die als das Ganze selbst und damit ebenso sehr als Bestimmungen gesetzt sind, ist alsdann der Begriff.
            %p §1234 This relation in its immediate Notion is the relation of substance and accidents, the immediate vanishing and becoming of the absolute illusory being within itself. When substance determines itself to being-for-self over against an other, or the absolute relation determines itself as real, then we have the relation of causality. Lastly, when this as self-relating passes over into reciprocity, the absolute relation is also posited in accordance with the determinations it contains; this posited unity of itself in its determinations which are posited as themselves the whole, but equally as determinations, is then the Notion.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              accidence
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/accidence"} ?
          %h5#TheRelationOfSubstantiality Das Verhaeltniß der Sustantialitaet / The relation of substantiality
          %blockquote
            %p#1235 §1235 Absolute necessity is absolute relation because it is not being as such, but being that is because it is, being as absolute self-mediation. This being is substance; as the final unity of essence and being it is the being in all being; it is neither the unreflected immediate, nor an abstract being standing behind Existence and Appearance, but it is immediate actuality itself and this as absolute reflectedness-into-self, as a subsisting in and for itself. Substance as this unity of being and reflection is essentially the reflective movement [Scheinen] and positedness of itself. The reflective movement is the reflective movement that is self-related, and it is thus that it is; this being is substance as such. Conversely, this being is only the positedness that is identical with itself, and as such it is totality in the form of illusory being, accidentality.
          %p This means that
          %ul
            %li
              %em substance
              is the unity of being and reflection
          %p
            recall also the announcement of substance in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#1161"} §1161
          %blockquote
            %p#1238 §1238 Substance, as this identity of the reflective movement, is the totality of the whole and embraces accidentality within it, and accidentality is the whole substance itself.
          %p Since above we identified
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the logic of being with the system of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            %li
              %p
                the logic of essence with the theory of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} (infinity,1)-topos
          %p
            the union of the two is the totality of the whole, the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (infinity,1)-topos
          %p
            Therefore we label in the
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Process"} Process
            the
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            as “substance”.
          %p
            Notice here that every (higher) topos has two geometric interpretations (see at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "topos#Idea"} topos – Idea
            %em category of spaces
            and on the other hand as one single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            (see there) itself, the latter at least if it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            or more generally a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} structured (infinity,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "differential+cohesion#StructureSheaves"} differential cohesion – structure sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            may naturally be interpreted as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                spaces modeled on the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                spaces built out of the substance
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            A modern imagery may be suggestive here: consider this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            coded (as has partially been done already, see the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive%20homotopy%20type%20theory#References"} cohesive HoTT – References
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            = succeed "," do
              %em software
            %em substance
            on which the world, nature, is to be modeled.
          %p
            This imagery also seems to be well suited to do away with the notorious issue with whether
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza%27s+system"} Spinoza's substance
            is “material”. No, it is not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            as in the nature which “runs” on it, still, it is in evident sense the substance out of which this nature is formed, but, if one wishes, a substance of a more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idealism"} idealistic
            form that plain matter.
          %p
            Compare this to the statement of
            %a{:href => "#1287"} §1287
            that this
            %em substance
            is the substance of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza%27s+system"} Spinoza's system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza"} Spinoza
            introduces as
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "Spinoza%27s+system#PerSubstantiamIntelligo"}> Per Substantiam Intelligo
              – By substance I understand what is in itself and is conceived through itself, i.e., that whose concept does not require the concept of another thing, from which it must be formed.
          %p Back in the imagery of a computer algebra software for formalized fundamental physics, this is indeed the situation after the moment of booting the system and seeing the green prompt on an empty black screen. Nothing has been introduced yet on the basis of anything, all we have is the software kernel running, from which now everything is to be formed. In this imagery, Spinoza’s
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "Spinoza%27s+system#Prop15"} Prop 15
              Whatever is, is in the substance, and nothing can be or be conceived without the substance.
          %p seems clear.
          %blockquote
            %p#1236 §1236 Die Bewegung der Accidentalität stellt daher an jedem ihrer Momente das Scheinen der Kategorien des Seyns und der Reflexions-Bestimmungen des Wesens in einander dar.
            %p §1236 The movement of accidentality therefore exhibits in each of its moments the illusory showing in one another of the categories of being and of the reflective determinations of essence.
            %p#1237 §1237 Diese Bewegung der Accidentalität ist die Aktuosität der Substanz, als ruhiges Hervorgehen ihrer selbst.
            %p §1237 This movement of accidentality is the actuosity of substance as a tranquil coming forth of itself.
          %p
            Where for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            determines a category of being, as discussed above, we may ask if it happens to be, accidentally, exhibited by a type/object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
            in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi loc
                    %mi 𝔸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643; \simeq loc_{\mathbb{A}}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}\to\ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
            has the interpretation of theing the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"} smooth infinity-groupoids
          %blockquote
            %p#1238 §1238 Die Substanz als diese Identität des Scheinens ist die Totalität des Ganzen, und begreift die Accidentalität in sich, und die Accidentalität ist die ganze Substanz selbst. Der Unterschied ihrer in die einfache Identität des Seyns, und in den Wechsel der Accidenzen an derselben ist eine Form ihres Scheins. Jenes ist die formlose Substanz des Vorstellens, dem der Schein sich nicht als Schein bestimmt hat, sondern das als an einem Absoluten an solcher unbestimmten Identität festhält, die keine Wahrheit hat, nur die Bestimmtheit der unmittelbaren Wirklichkeit oder ebenso des Ansichseyns oder der Möglichkeit ist;—Formbestimmungen, welche in die Accidentalität fallen.
            %p §1238 Substance, as this identity of the reflective movement, is the totality of the whole and embraces accidentality within it, and accidentality is the whole substance itself. The differentiation of itself into the simple identity of being and the flux of accidents in it, is a form of its illusory being. The former is the formless substance of ordinary thinking for which illusory being has not determined itself as illusory being, but which clings to such an indeterminate identity as an absolute, an identity which has no truth and is only the determinateness of immediate actuality or equally of the in-itself or possibility — form determinations which fall into accidentality.
          %h5#the_relation_of_causality The relation of causality
          %h5#reciprocity Reciprocity
          %h2#die_lehre_vom_begriff__the_doctrine_of_the_notion
            %strong Die Lehre vom Begriff
